            "text": "Makes a tensor of ones the same shape as the tensor you send it.\n\nUse it when a neutral tensor - weights that change nothing, a mask that lets everything through - must match data you already have. Times a number, it becomes a constant of that shape. Typing the shape into t.ones breaks the moment the data changes size; t.ones_like follows it.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.ones_like <dtype> <device>\nNo arguments are needed.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.ones_like\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor whose SHAPE to copy. Anything arriving here makes the node send - a stream of tensors gives a stream of outputs, each matching the one that came in. A list, NumPy array or number has a shape too and works the same way: a list of 5 numbers gives 5 values, a list of lists a 2-D tensor, a single number a 1-element tensor. A bang sends a new tensor in the shape last received.\n\ndtype (menu):\nThe number type. float32 is the default and what most torch operations want. The menu also offers float16, int64, uint8, bool, complex64 and complex128; 'float' is the same as float32, and each name appears a second time with a 'torch.' prefix, which means the same thing. There is no 64-bit float here.\n\ndevice (menu):\nWhere the tensor is made: cpu (the default), and mps or cuda when the machine has them. Tensors on different devices cannot be combined, so keep a chain on one device.\n\nrequires_grad (checkbox):\nRecords operations for automatic differentiation. Leave it off unless you are training something. Only float and complex tensors can carry it: ticked with an integer or bool dtype, the node raises an error and sends nothing.\n\nA dtype or device name can also be typed as an argument, after the numbers - for example t.ones_like int64.\nNOTE - ONLY THE SHAPE IS COPIED:\nPyTorch's own torch.ones_like also copies the input's dtype and device. This node does not: the output is always the dtype and device set on the node (float32 on cpu unless you change them). Feed it a float16 tensor on mps and you get float32 on cpu back. Set dtype and device here to match if the two tensors will meet later.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor of ones:\nA tensor the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA metro clicks t.linspace, whose ramp (top strip) feeds t.ones_like. The bottom strip is the result: the same number of cells, all 1 (all yellow). Drag steps on t.linspace: both strips divide together, and t.info shows the shape following.\n\nRELATED:\nt.ones makes ones in a shape you type. t.zeros_like and t.rand_like copy the shape the same way with zeros or random values. t.full fills with any value."
        },
